+# Control-law fixed-assignment diagnostic specification
+
+Status: `DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM`.
+
+The measurement uses the eight real `V025_PROBE_R2/world/{1,2}:step/{0..3}`
+anchors at the sealed 1.66 degree one-sided half-power angle.  For each of the
+SEALED and STRICT provisioning rules, the user-to-beam assignment is the
+correctly paired `J_from_first` committed assignment recorded by the final
+beam-width receipt.
+
+Acceptance criteria:
+
+1. Independently replay C1 over all 48 boundaries and require its bits,
+   joules, served count, and target count to match the committed receipt.
+2. Hold every C1 user-to-beam assignment fixed.  Search only the per-slot
+   transmitted ACM mode (including OFF where C1 failed) and RF power.
+3. Never reduce a C1-decoded link's mode.  This conservatively preserves the
+   service guard and rate-target attainment; verify both endpoint counts.
+4. For each discrete mode vector solve the realised, co-channel coupled
+   minimum-power equations directly, reject cap violations, and record
+   residual/SINR checks.
+5. Label the bounded deterministic two-order coordinate search as best-known,
+   not certified.  Report anchor and pooled C1/C2/C3 plus exact additive C4
+   decompositions by C1 cap status, occupancy, elevation, off-axis angle, and
+   interference-component size.
+6. State that the C2 oracle uses joint realised coupling/mode information and
+   is not itself an implementable causal rule; quantify the affected
+   component sizes for C5.
+7. Run with one Python process and one numerical-library thread.  Do not edit
+   production physics or any sibling workspace.
+
